  <text>
    <body>
      <div>
        <head>In the directories</head>
        <p>The Royal Hotel is in the directories in <ref target="civic?id=Victoria%20Street">Victoria Street</ref> from 1857 to 1920. It is No. 33 from 1859 to 1861, No. 142 from 1890, and Nos 142–146 in 1920. In 1920 it is also listed at No. 1 <ref target="civic?id=Madeline%20Street">Madeline Street</ref>, so it seems to have been on the Madeline Street corner. It is not listed in 1875, the year the directory lists <ref target="civic?id=Payne%27s%20Royal%20hotel">Payne's Royal hotel</ref> under John Payne in Victoria Street; the two names are kept apart here.</p>
        <p>The directories list the proprietors as Samuel John Davidson (1857 to 1859), Richard Richardson (1860), William C. Conroy (1861 to 1867), H. Austin (1868), R. Glass (1869), H. Anscombe (1870), John Plomer (1871 to 1874), M. J. Cook (1876), E. Burton (1877), Robert Beeston (1878 to 1885), T. Curran (1890), F. Clark (1895), <ref target="civic?id=Ellen%20O%27Sullivan">Mrs Ellen O'Sullivan</ref> (1900 and 1905), Mrs Lena Holman (1910), Henry Higgins (1915) and Ernest A. Simms (1920).</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <head>Neighbours</head>
        <p>The <ref target="civic?id=Royal%20Hotel%20Stables">Royal Hotel Stables</ref> are next to the hotel from 1859 to 1868. George Sinclair, ironmonger, is at No. 41 in 1859 and 1860, and David Middleton, saddler, next door in 1861 and 1862. <ref target="civic?id=Charles%20Nettleton">Charles Nettleton</ref>, photographer, is next door from 1867 to 1874, and Frank Silk's livery stables in 1870 and 1871. Edmund Ashley, coach builders' ironmonger, is next to the hotel from 1878 to 1915, at No. 138 from 1890. The Royal Horse &amp; Carriage Co. is listed beside it in 1885, with stables at No. 150 under H. Mitchell in 1890, and the <ref target="civic?id=Carlton%20Brewery%20Ltd">Carlton Brewery Ltd</ref> at No. 150 in 1900 and 1905.</p>
      </div>
